ll, and no epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies`.\n27\t\n28\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n29\tAll three epics are Implementation type and correctly carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric) with `investigation_signals: null` — no rubric mismatch. AI-signals rationale files exist for all three epics, and every rationale matches its frontmatter value and is substantiated by strategy/architecture-context content. E001's signals (fully specified change, existing config path, no open questions, corroborated architecture claims) are consistent with the strategy. E002's `human_process_gates: 0` is explicitly reasoned (no formal approval gate, but test-cluster/environment coordination) — a defensible neutral for validation work. E003's `human_process_gates: -1` correctly reflects the Red Hat docs peer/technical review process and matches the epic's own technical-review acceptance criterion. No signal value contradicts the rubric conditions.\n30\t\n31\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n32\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria derived from the strategy. E001 uses Given/When/Then ACs covering fresh installs, upgrade preservation of explicit values, migration of clusters relying on the old default, and the override path as the documented rollback mechanism for the behavior change (the rule-mandated rollback/feature-flag AC for a replacement-style change, correctly noting the existing override mechanism obviates a feature flag). E002 has testable validation ACs plus a concrete validation-report deliverable. E003 includes the rule-mandated technical-review-before-publication AC for docs-authoring, plus accuracy validation against implemented behavior. No konflux-chain epics exist, so no build-pipeline AC applies. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID.\n33\t\n34\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n35\tThe full strategy scope is accounted for. HLR-1's two halves (new-default change; explicit-value preservation on upgrade) are both in E001's scope 